A||||14||Pretender||LOWER BALL JOINT INSTALL!!!????!!!
Z||000000||Pretender||07-12-99||01:45 AM||||oh great!, my mechanic can't get to my car untill next week!...but i need to get to work tomorrow!..how hard is this procedure and what tools would i need?.. i can't remember what the mech. did last time to fix the other side...it is the passenger side lower ball joint if that helps any...<br>
Z||000001||PaUL||07-12-99||02:09 AM||||You need to remove the lower control arm and have the balljoint pressed off. Thats the proper procedure but when I did it, I didnt have time for that so I just pounded the old one out with a hammer and a large socket, then pounded the new one in. They have a serated edge which matches the edge on the arm. If you have the time and another car, I think you should have it pressed out and new joint pressed on.
Z||000002||RICK||07-12-99||07:23 AM||||I used the hammer, about 2 or 3 good whacks and it fell on the floor, it wasnt that hard at all.  To get the new one in I used a large press to push it back in.<br>
Z||000003||Hock||07-12-99||12:09 PM||||Well, I did the ones on my Fiero and they were welded in the control arm. It took like a hour to get the damn things out. There is a company that makes a tool to remove and install them. It cost somewhere between $40-$200, depending where you get it. Insted of buying it I just hammered on them for a while, and trust me it is harder than it sounds.
Z||000004||mwbackus||07-12-99||11:11 PM||||Hammer?? Geesh.. Autozone has a ball joint press in its rental tool array that works just fine.  It requires a deposit, but other than that it is totally free to rent.  Considering how integral the ball joints are to the safe operation of the fiero, and the amount of force required to properly press the joint in, I would highly recommned either using this tool, or having them pressed in/out by a professional.  
Z||000005||PaUL||07-13-99||12:12 AM||||Thats what I said too but if dont have the time or another car....you do what you got to do.
